Denote by (\alpha) the angle trunsed by a clock. Namely, $\alpha = (\beta)$ = $360^\circ$ $\times$ (hour / 12 hours) - degree of the clock, where $\beta$ is the angle obtained by the hour hand with the minute hand at the sexagesimal count. \\
$(\alpha)^\circ = 360^\circ \times \left(\dfrac{\text{present hour during the sexagesimal count}}{12}\right) - (\text{present minute during the sexagesimal count})$. \\
$\quad$ or equivalently $(\alpha)^\circ = \dfrac{360}{12} \cdot (\alpha)^\circ + 60(\alpha)^\circ + (\alpha)^\circ = 360(\alpha)^\circ \quad \Rightarrow \quad (\alpha)^\circ = \left(\alpha \pmod{12}\right) \times \dfrac{360}{12}$.\end{tabular}

